<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basse-Terre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basse-Terre is the capital of the French overseas region of Guadeloupe, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, on the south-west coast of the island of Basse-Terre. Founded by French settlers in 1643, it was the seat of the colony's governors, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-a-pitre">Pointe-à-Pitre</a> became the main commercial port. It lies at the foot of the active volcano La Soufrière.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basse-terre.jpg" alt="Basse-Terre" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Jolivet · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monts_Cara%C3%AFbes_depuis_le_Fort_Louis_Delgr%C3%A8s_%C3%A0_Basse-Terre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cayo-style pottery, found from Grenada to <b>Basse-Terre</b> and possibly Saint Kitts and dated between AD 1000 and 1500, resembles the mainland Koriabo style and the Meillacoid and Chicoid styles of the Greater Antilles, while Suazan Troumassoid pottery grew in the islands from earlier Saladoid traditions.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>A royal governor ruled from <b>Basse-Terre</b>, and Pointe-à-Pitre grew into the main commercial port.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-guadeloupe">Colony of Guadeloupe</a></li></ul></section>
